WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Привет, Фрэнс!

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:06.320
Welcome to привет, русский, Episode 24, 414 мая 2026 года.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:09.700
I'm Olya, and we've got some great Russian words to learn today.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.040
Поехали, let's go!

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.240
Our word of the day is cosmos.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.180
Cosmos.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.900
It means space, just like the stars and planets all around us.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.120
This word sounds exactly like the English word cosmos, so you already know it.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.180
Can you say it with me? Repeat after me.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:24.820
Cosmos.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.960
Now, once more at natural speed.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:27.660
Cosmos.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.440
Here is how we use it.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.060
Я вижу звезды в космосе.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.040
That means I see stars in space.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.720
Now that you know Cosmos, let's build more words about the same theme.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.500
Our next word is звезда, звезда.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.520
It means star.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.320
Repeat after me, звезда.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:43.940
Now again, звезда.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.880
Imagine a star twinkling like a dazzling diamond, and you will remember it.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:50.160
We can say, это звезда очень яркая.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.160
That means the star is very bright.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:53.760
Our next word is Luna.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:53.860
Luna.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:54.380
Luna.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:55.660
It means moon.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:56.780
Repeat after me.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:57.220
Luna.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:58.480
And again, Luna.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.980
Think of the moon as the same friendly face you see in many stories.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.640
We can say, Luna, круглая и белая.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.080
That means the moon is round and white.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:08.740
Next comes planeta.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:09.680
Planeta.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:10.820
It means planet.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:11.960
Repeat after me.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:12.680
Planeta.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.380
And once more, planeta.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.300
It sounds just like the English word planet, so it is an instant match.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.720
We can say, Земля это планета.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.320
That means Earth is a planet.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.500
Our next word is Raketa.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:25.320
Raketa.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:26.400
It means rocket.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:27.500
Repeat after me.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:28.220
Raketa.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:29.640
And again, Raketa.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.820
Picture a rocket blasting off with a big bush, and you will never forget it.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.260
We can say, Raketa lit its cosmos.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:38.700
That means the rocket flies into space.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.540
Next we have astronaut.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:42.000
Astronaut.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.480
It means astronaut.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:44.580
Repeat after me.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:45.440
Astronaut.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:46.580
And again, astronaut.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:47.900
Astronaut.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.220
And now it is like navigator, so it is a star traveler.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.420
We can say, astronaut на луне.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.740
That means the astronaut is on the moon.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:57.700
Our next word is telescope.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:58.880
Telescope.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:01:59.800
It means telescope.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:00.980
Repeat after me.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:01.780
Telescope.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.140
And once more, telescope.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.900
It looks and sounds almost exactly like the English word, so you already know it.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.720
We can say, yes, matter of telescope.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.700
That means, I look through the telescope.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.380
Finally, we have Sputnik.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:14.200
Sputnik.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:15.160
It means satellite.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:16.140
Repeat after me.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:16.840
Sputnik.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.260
And again, Sputnik.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:21.860
Think of a Sputnik as a friendly companion orbiting with you.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.300
We can say, Sputnik cruzит вокруг Земли.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.760
That means the satellite circles around Earth.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.100
Now for a quick grammar bite that will help you use these words right away.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.100
Russian nouns have three genders, masculine, feminine, and neuter.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.520
This decides how small words like this change their endings.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.280
For example, we say,

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.340
Etat's vis-da, because vis-da is feminine.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.980
We say, Etat's Sputnik, because Sputnik is masculine.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.600
And we say Etat-Nieba, because Nieba is neuter.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.940
Unlike English, which uses the same THE or A for everything.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.740
Russian shows the gender through the ending sounds.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.300
Now you can point at things and say Etat, for neuter words like the moon.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:03.880
And it will feel natural.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.740
Russians have a special love for space because their countries send the very first satellite

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:11.380
and the first human Yuri Gagarin into orbit.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.940
Many families still watch old clips of these launches on Cosmonautics day

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.300
every April 12.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.180
They celebrate with stories and drawings of rockets,

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.440
keeping the excitement alive for children and adults alike.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.680
For our practice time, here is a fun challenge.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.260
If you are a kid, draw your own rocket and three stars,

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.320
then point to each one and say the Russian word out loud three times.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:38.120
If you are an adult, look up at the night sky or a picture of it

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.380
and describe what you see using at least three new words in simple sentences like

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:47.260
Yavijos Visdu, you just practice real sentences and that is wonderful progress.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.440
Now, here is something really cool about one of today's work.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.640
Want to know a secret about Sputnik?

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:56.700
Did you know that the Russian word Sputnik and the English word Sputnik

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.020
are actually the same word?

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.060
They both come from the idea of a traveling companion.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.140
This word started in old Slavic languages, meaning a fellow traveler

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.800
or someone who goes along with you.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.960
It rocketed into global fame in 1957

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.300
when the Soviet Union launched the first artificial satellite

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.840
and simply called it Sputnik.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.600
English speakers borrowed the word directly

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.700
because nothing else captured that exciting new invention.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.720
But be careful because in everyday Russian Sputnik can also just

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.080
mean a human friend traveling with you, not only a space machine.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.980
Next time you see the word satellite or hear about old space news,

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.040
remember it is cousins with Sputnik.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:38.060
They both come from the simple idea of keeping someone company on a journey.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.500
That connection is your secret shortcut to remembering it forever.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:43.760
Молодец, that means well done.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.660
Remember, every expert started as a beginner practicing today's words out loud.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.640
Even just once and you'll be amazed how fast you learn.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:52.760
See you next time.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.480
Пока, that's Russian for buy.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.760
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.300
Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
